{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Implemented and pushed as Gerrit change 2550 (https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2550), from a worktree off origin/main.\n\nAC1: _registry.py 800 -> 685 lines (115 headroom); it no longer appears in the >780 near-cap census at all. AC4: _registry_checks.py is 170 lines, above the 100-line floor.\n\nAC2: an __all__ in _registry.py pins the eight externally-consumed names from the census (ROUTES, Route, validate, route_for, derive_policy_sets, ADAPTER_KINDS, INIT_POLICIES, KNOWN_CAPABILITIES). Two mutations verify it: removing Finding from __all__ fails test_declared_surface_covers_the_split_names, and dropping the Finding re-export import fails test_public_surface_resolves with the exact missing name.\n\nAC3/AC5: behaviour identical. Snapshotted route names, aliases, all 17 derived policy sets, validate() findings and the three vocabularies from the UNMODIFIED pre-split tree (a separate worktree at origin/main), then compared post-split: identical across all seven dimensions.\n\nDesign note on import direction, which the plan did not settle. A naive extraction cycles, because validate() defaults to ROUTES while the checks would live outside _registry. Resolved by keeping validate (and its default) in _registry and moving only the checks, which take the route table as a parameter — so _registry imports _registry_checks and never the reverse. Route is needed for annotations only, so it is imported under TYPE_CHECKING. The three vocabularies moved with the checks because the checks are their only runtime consumer (their other mentions in _registry were comments).\n\nThe committed test targets the failure mode an import-only test would miss: a check that moved but was left out of _CHECKS makes the registry validate CLEAN because the rule stopped running, not because the tree is good. So each of the nine rules is exercised independently through the public validate against a crafted violating table, plus a negative control. Dropping _check_capabilities from _CHECKS turns two tests red; dropping _check_execution turns five red.\n\nProcess note. During mutation testing I ran git checkout -- on _registry_checks.py while it was still UNTRACKED, so that revert silently failed; the next checkout then restored _registry.py to its pre-split state and wiped the split. I re-applied it, staged all three files first so reverts actually work, and re-ran the three mutations cleanly — the earlier __all__ result was contaminated by the unreverted first mutation and was discarded rather than reported.\n\nGates: make lint and make typecheck clean; 1127 passed / 3 skipped in tests/unit -k \"registry or capabilities or dispatch or cli\".\n\nThis unblocks F11 (elfin-decagonal-polarbear), which needs Route headroom."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788401992981707001,"uuid":"9d9f0fea-7fa1-48b9-af81-c6d9a4bd9fe9"}